# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
#
# v4l2loopback -- vendored from Ubuntu's v4l2loopback-dkms packaging.
#
# WHY THIS IS IN THE TREE RATHER THAN BUILT OUT OF TREE
# This kernel has CONFIG_MODVERSIONS unset, so a module is accepted on vermagic
# alone -- essentially the release string, which embeds `git describe`. An
# out-of-tree .ko is therefore keyed to ONE commit and stops loading at the next
# kernel change. That is a dated failure, not a risk: the camera would simply
# disappear on the next boot flash. As an ordinary =m symbol the module is built
# and installed with the rest of the module tree and cannot fall out of step.
#
# PROVENANCE -- Ubuntu's packaging, NOT upstream's tarball
# Upstream's release does not carry the client-usage event that the userspace
# bridge depends on for consumer detection; Ubuntu's does, as a v4l2-relayd
# compatibility patch. Taking upstream would silently remove it.
#
#   source  : v4l2loopback-dkms 0.15.3-1ubuntu2  (apt-get download)
#   files   : v4l2loopback.c, v4l2loopback.h, v4l2loopback_formats.h, PRISTINE
#   dropped : Kbuild (one line, obj-m), Makefile and dkms.conf -- packaging only
#
# The revision matters and the version string cannot express it: 0.15.3-1ubuntu2
# added fix-buffer-queue-management.patch for V4L2 spec compliance, and both
# revisions report `version: 0.15.3` to modinfo. This source was confirmed
# byte-identical to the out-of-tree module it replaces by comparing `srcversion`,
# an MD5 over the source files: 7B773B9EB594AD896DAD368 for both, with identical
# disassembly across all 6158 lines.
#
# To re-vendor: apt-get download v4l2loopback-dkms, copy the three files over,
# and diff. Do not edit them here.

config VIDEO_V4L2LOOPBACK
	tristate "V4L2 loopback device"
	depends on VIDEO_DEV
	help
	  A virtual V4L2 capture device that presents whatever a producer
	  writes into it as an ordinary camera.

	  On this device it is the only mechanism that makes the camera
	  reachable by every application. Mainline qcom-camss is RDI-only, so
	  the real capture nodes hand out raw Bayer that nothing can display,
	  and a picture only exists after libcamera's software ISP -- which
	  leaves the camera visible solely as a PipeWire node. Browsers can opt
	  in to that; ordinary V4L2 applications cannot, and neither can a snap
	  or a flatpak, because the opt-in lives in host userspace that a
	  sandbox cannot see. A genuine kernel device node is what crosses the
	  sandbox boundary.

	  The userspace half is camera-bridge, installed by the rootfs recipe.

	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module will be
	  called v4l2loopback.
